--      	  f = x^10
--     	  b = globalBFunction f
-- 	  factorBFunction b
--      	  ///,
--      SeeAlso => { "bFunction", "factorBFunction", "generalB", "globalB" }
--      }

doc ///
  Key
    (globalBFunction, RingElement)
    globalBFunction
  Headline
    global $b$-function (also known as the Bernstein-Sato polynomial)
  Usage
    b = globalBFunction f
  Inputs
    f:RingElement
      a polynomial
  Outputs
    b:RingElement
      the $b$-function $b(s)$ in $\QQ[s]$
  Description
    Text
      @BOLD "Definition."@ Let $D = A_{2n}(K) = K\langle x_1,\ldots,x_n,dx_1,\ldots,dx_n\rangle$
      be a Weyl algebra.
      The Bernstein-Sato polynomial of a polynomial $f$ is defined
      to be the monic generator of the ideal of all polynomials
      $b(s)$ in $K[s]$ such that
      $b(s) f^s = Q(s,x,d) f^{s+1}$ where $Q$ lives in $D[s]$.

      @BOLD "Algorithm."@
      Let $I_f = D\langle t,dt\rangle \cdot \langle t-f, dx_1+\partial f/\partial x_1 \cdot dt, \ldots, dx_n+\partial f/\partial x_n \cdot dt\rangle$.
      Let $B(s) = \text{bFunction}(I, \{1, 0, \ldots, 0\})$
      where 1 is the weight that corresponds to $dt$.
      Then the global $b$-function is $b_f = B(-s-1)$.
    Example
      R = QQ[x]
      f = x^10
      b = globalBFunction f
      factorBFunction b
  SeeAlso
    bFunction
    factorBFunction
    generalB
    globalB
///

